WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.580
Design begins with the investigations the workstation must support.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.695
A rapid incident-response task has different needs from long-term due diligence,

00:00:07.695 --> 00:00:10.906
public-infrastructure review, journalism, or academic research.

00:00:10.906 --> 00:00:14.550
Define typical case size, deadlines, source types, sensitivity,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.210
and reporting expectations.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.620
Identify privacy and data-handling requirements.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.960
Determine whether case material can remain on one device,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.780
must use approved organizational storage, or needs restricted access.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:29.095
Record legal, contractual, regulatory, and policy constraints before selecting tools.

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:32.950
Estimate storage for notes, public documents, approved captures, archives,

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.855
exported records, and backups.

00:00:34.855 --> 00:00:36.900
Video and image-heavy cases grow quickly.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.310
Capacity planning should include working space, retention, encrypted backup,

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:42.780
and enough free space for reliable operation.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:43.970
Map the browser workflow.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.605
Analysts may need one or more dedicated profiles, consistent bookmarks,

00:00:47.605 --> 00:00:51.670
approved extensions, capture tools, and a way to record sources as they work.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.940
Personal accounts should not be part of the default research environment.

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.420
Define case organization and evidence expectations.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.700
A workstation should support source logs, structured notes, timestamps, file naming,

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.424
raw-versus-working separation, and links between observations and judgments.

00:01:05.424 --> 00:01:09.010
Auditability should be designed in rather than reconstructed after the report.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:11.020
Choose requirements that can be maintained.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.735
Reliability, updates, recovery,

00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:17.140
and usability are more valuable than elaborate isolation that users cannot operate.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.635
Document the intended configuration, responsible owner, maintenance schedule,

00:01:20.635 --> 00:01:23.060
and conditions that would justify changing the design.
